Take $x\ne y$ with $\pi(x)q(x,y)>0$ (otherwise both sides below are $0$ by the same argument). Then
\begin{align}
\pi(x)K(x,y)&=\pi(x)q(x,y)\min\Big\{1,\frac{\pi(y)q(y,x)}{\pi(x)q(x,y)}\Big\}\\
&=\min\big\{\pi(x)q(x,y),\;\pi(y)q(y,x)\big\}.
\end{align}
The right-hand side is unchanged when $x$ and $y$ are swapped, so it also equals $\pi(y)q(y,x)\alpha(y,x)=\pi(y)K(y,x)$. Hence the Metropolis-Hastings kernel satisfies detailed balance with respect to $\pi$ (the rejection probability only affects the diagonal, where detailed balance is trivial), and by part (a)(ii) $\pi$ is stationary. $\blacksquare$

By the product rule, $\log p(y)=\log p(y,\theta)-\log p(\theta\mid y)$ for every $\theta$. Take the expectation under $q$; the left side does not depend on $\theta$, so
\begin{align}
\log p(y)&=\E_q[\log p(y,\theta)]-\E_q[\log p(\theta\mid y)]\\
&=\underbrace{\E_q[\log p(y,\theta)]-\E_q[\log q(\theta)]}_{\text{ELBO}(q)}+\underbrace{\E_q[\log q(\theta)]-\E_q[\log p(\theta\mid y)]}_{\operatorname{KL}(q\,\|\,p(\cdot\mid y))}.
\end{align}
Since $\operatorname{KL}\ge0$, $\text{ELBO}(q)\le\log p(y)$. The left side $\log p(y)$ does not depend on $q$, so a larger ELBO means a smaller KL divergence: maximising the ELBO over the variational family is equivalent to minimising $\operatorname{KL}(q\|p(\cdot\mid y))$. $\blacksquare$
